		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">40</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Following are some hints to put the logic in words.

1. As there are 1000 elements in the array, it will be difficult for me to remember all the elements of the array, so I will write all the elements of the array in paper.
2. Now I will start from first element of the array and start counting how many numbers in the array are greater than that of the element. 
3. After comapring with all the elements I will compare the value of count with the element value and if they are equal that means I dont have to check for any other element and my output is 1
4. If count value and element value is not equal then I will repeat same process for next element
5. So if there is no element exist that means my output is -1.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">180.90714514647703</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">3225.2555506986037</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">348.4091545813991</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">254.482998015921</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">Following are some hints to put the logic in words.

1. As there are 1000 elements in the array, it will be difficult for me to remember all the elements of the array, so I will write all the elements of the array in paper.
2. Now I will start from first element of the array and start counting how many numbers in the array are greater than that of the element. 
3. After comapring with all the elements I will compare the value of count with the element value and if they are equal that means I dont have to check for any other element and my output is 1
4. If count value and element value is not equal then I will repeat same process for next element
5. So if there is no element exist that means my output is -1.</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontSize" type="int">12</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontName" type="String">Dialog</attribute>
				<attribute key="configuration" type="String">CroppingLabel</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentWidth" type="double">348.4091545813991</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentHeight" type="double">217.3125</attribute>
				<attribute key="autoSizePolicy" type="String">node_width</attribute>
				<attribute key="anchor" type="String">c</attribute>
			</section>
		</section>
		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">41</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Following is what I came up with.

We have 2 major phases in the implementation.
Phase 1: One is to read the elements of the array.
Phase 2: Second is finding the noble integer.

1. Read the length of the array
2. Create an empty array.
3. Read the next value from input
4. Append it to the array
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you read all the elements of the array.
Phase 2
6. Create a variable i and initialize it to 0.
7. Create a variable called count and initialize it to 0.
8. Create a variable called currVal and initialize it to value at index i of the array.
9. Now start comparing element of array, if it is greater than currVal or not, if yes
9.1 Increase the value of count by 1, otherwise
9.2 Do nothing 
10. Repeat the steps 9, 9.1 and 9.2 till we reach the end of array.
11. After reaching the end of array, compare the value of count with the value of currVal.
11.1 If both are equal then break out of the loop and display 1, otherwise
11.2 Go to the next element of array by increasing the value of i by 1
12. Repeat step 7, 8, 9, 9.1, 9.2, 10, 11, 11.1 and 11.2 untill we reach end of the array.
13. After reaching the end of array display -1, since there is no integer P in the array for which there are exactly P numbers greater than P.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">-3.9077372706250344</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">3602.8835577011646</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">746.6917705246622</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">383.3706722391988</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">Following is what I came up with.

We have 2 major phases in the implementation.
Phase 1: One is to read the elements of the array.
Phase 2: Second is finding the noble integer.

1. Read the length of the array
2. Create an empty array.
3. Read the next value from input
4. Append it to the array
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you read all the elements of the array.
Phase 2
6. Create a variable i and initialize it to 0.
7. Create a variable called count and initialize it to 0.
8. Create a variable called currVal and initialize it to value at index i of the array.
9. Now start comparing element of array, if it is greater than currVal or not, if yes
9.1 Increase the value of count by 1, otherwise
9.2 Do nothing 
10. Repeat the steps 9, 9.1 and 9.2 till we reach the end of array.
11. After reaching the end of array, compare the value of count with the value of currVal.
11.1 If both are equal then break out of the loop and display 1, otherwise
11.2 Go to the next element of array by increasing the value of i by 1
12. Repeat step 7, 8, 9, 9.1, 9.2, 10, 11, 11.1 and 11.2 untill we reach end of the array.
13. After reaching the end of array display -1, since there is no integer P in the array for which there are exactly P numbers greater than P.</attribute>
